_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 20
---
order: 20
---
short_label: III.20
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: In a circle the angle at the center is double the angle at the circumference when the angles have the same circumference as base.
----
proof:

Let {ABC} be a circle, let the angle {BEC} be an angle at its center, and the angle {BAC} an angle at the circumference, and let them have the same circumference {BC} as base.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="280" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.20 diagram" src="propIII20.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The proposition is about angles, and Joyce's figure draws none — his
    // diagram has the circle, the chords and the diameter. The markers below
    // are the whole addition. showAngles defaults to false (0.9.0+), so they
    // are hidden in the resting figure and arrive only with the walk or on
    // hover; the four transparent lines and the transparent arc draw nothing,
    // so the static figure is pixel-unchanged.
    //
    // Colour: each angle at the circumference shares its colour with its
    // double at the centre — orange for EAB (= EBA) and BEF, teal for EAC and
    // FEC, magenta for the pair the proposition is about, BAC and BEC. The
    // second case reuses the scheme: orange EDC / GEC, teal EDB / GEB,
    // magenta BDC / BEC. Named colours give an OPAQUE face (parseColor has no
    // alpha), so at each vertex the containing wedge is declared BEFORE the
    // wedges it contains and the faces pass paints the parts on top of it.
    // Radii are pinned by hand for the same reason: the auto ring-stepping
    // (#103) orders by span across ALL markers at a vertex, and E carries
    // five. The override at D is 28 rather than 34 because the radius is
    // clamped to 0.45 x the shorter arm, and DC is only ~64px.
    //
    // Joyce's AF is one element, and so is DG. "Join AE, and draw it through
    // to F" is therefore a single straightedge stroke A -> E -> F; AE / EF
    // (and DE / EG) exist as transparent hover targets so the captions can
    // name the halves.
    var CIRC  = ["ABC","A","B","C"];
    var ANG   = CIRC.concat(["E","EB","EC","AB","AC","angBEC","angBAC"]);
    var BASE  = ANG.concat(["arcBC"]);
    var DIAM  = BASE.concat(["AF","F","AE","EF"]);
    var ISO   = DIAM.concat(["angEAB","angEBA"]);
    var EXT1  = ISO.concat(["angBEF"]);
    var EXT2  = EXT1.concat(["angFEC","angEAC"]);
    // Second case. The first case's markers come off, BAC included; BEC
    // stays, since it is what the second case is about too.
    var CASE2 = CIRC.concat(["E","EB","EC","AB","AC","angBEC","arcBC",
                             "AF","F","AE","EF","D","DB","DC","angBDC"]);
    var PROD  = CASE2.concat(["DG","G","DE","EG"]);
    var SIM1  = PROD.concat(["angGEC","angEDC"]);
    var SIM2  = SIM1.concat(["angGEB","angEDB"]);
    var DONE  = SIM2.concat(["angBAC"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.20",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;40,110",
            "F;point;free;240,170",
            "E;point;midpoint;A,F;black;green",
            "ABC;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;black;random",
            "B;point;circleSlider;ABC,190,210",
            "C;point;circleSlider;ABC,220,80",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "EC;line;connect;E,C",
            "EB;line;connect;E,B",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F",
            "D;point;circleSlider;ABC,170,40",
            "DC;line;connect;D,C",
            "DB;line;connect;D,B",
            "G;point;extend;D,E,D,E",
            "DG;line;connect;D,G",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — AF and DG already draw these.
            "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "EG;line;connect;E,G;0;0;0",
            // The circumference BC the two angles stand on. F is on the
            // circle (E is the midpoint of AF and the radius is EA), so it
            // serves as the through-point of the minor arc.
            "arcBC;sector;arc;B,F,C;0;0;0;0",
            // Angle markers, containing wedge first at each vertex.
            "angGEC;sector;angleMarker;E,G,C,46;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angBEC;sector;angleMarker;E,B,C,34;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angGEB;sector;angleMarker;E,G,B,34;0;0;teal;teal",
            "angBEF;sector;angleMarker;E,B,F,22;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angFEC;sector;angleMarker;E,F,C,22;0;0;teal;teal",
            "angBAC;sector;angleMarker;A,B,C,34;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angEAB;sector;angleMarker;A,E,B,22;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angEAC;sector;angleMarker;A,E,C,22;0;0;teal;teal",
            "angEBA;sector;angleMarker;B,E,A,22;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angEDC;sector;angleMarker;D,E,C,28;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angEDB;sector;angleMarker;D,E,B,22;0;0;teal;teal",
            "angBDC;sector;angleMarker;D,B,C,22;0;0;magenta;magenta"
        ],
        // Draggables (checked against the bundle: A, F, B, C, D) are
        // auto-unioned into every slide. F arrives with "draw it through to
        // F" and D with the second case, so both are deferred; A, B and C are
        // the given circle's letters. E (midpoint) and G (extend) are derived
        // and NOT auto-unioned, so each is listed in the sets above.
        deferDraggables: ["F","D"],
        aliases: { "EA": "AE", "BE": "EB", "CE": "EC", "FA": "AF",
                   "ED": "DE", "GE": "EG", "GD": "DG", "BD": "DB", "CD": "DC",
                   "BC": "arcBC", "CB": "arcBC",
                   "BEC": "angBEC", "CEB": "angBEC",
                   "BAC": "angBAC", "CAB": "angBAC",
                   "EAB": "angEAB", "BAE": "angEAB",
                   "EBA": "angEBA", "ABE": "angEBA",
                   "BEF": "angBEF", "FEB": "angBEF",
                   "FEC": "angFEC", "CEF": "angFEC",
                   "EAC": "angEAC", "CAE": "angEAC",
                   "BDC": "angBDC", "CDB": "angBDC",
                   "GEC": "angGEC", "CEG": "angGEC",
                   "EDC": "angEDC", "CDE": "angEDC",
                   "GEB": "angGEB", "BEG": "angGEB",
                   "EDB": "angEDB", "BDE": "angEDB" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”), split at its seams.
            { text: "“In a circle the angle at the center is double the angle at the circumference”",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: [] },
            { text: "“when the angles have the same circumference as base.”",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.20" }] },
            { text: "Let {ABC} be a circle,",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: ["ABC"] },
            { text: "let the angle {BEC} be an angle at its center, and the angle {BAC} an angle at the circumference,",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angBEC", "angBAC"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angBEC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angBAC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and let them have the same circumference {BC} as base.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["arcBC"] },
            { text: "I say that the angle {BEC} is double the angle {BAC}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["angBEC","angBAC"] },
            { text: "Join {AE}, and draw it through to {F}.",
              visible: DIAM,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "AF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "join AE",              ref: "I.Post.1" },
                  { claim: "produce it through to F", ref: "I.Post.2" } ] },
            { text: "Then, since {EA} equals {EB}, the angle {EAB} also equals the angle {EBA}.",
              visible: ISO,
              highlighted: ["AE","EB"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angEAB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angEBA", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "EA and EB are radii",                    ref: "I.Def.15" },
                  { claim: "the base angles of the isosceles triangle EAB", ref: "I.5" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the angles the angles {EAB} and {EBA} is double the angle {EAB}.",
              visible: ISO,
              highlighted: ["angEAB","angEBA"] },
            { text: "But the angle {BEF} equals the sum of the angles {EAB} and {EBA}, therefore the angle {BEF}, is also double the angle {EAB}.",
              visible: EXT1,
              highlighted: ["angEAB","angEBA"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angBEF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the exterior angle of the triangle EAB", ref: "I.32" } ] },
            { text: "For the same reason the angle {FEC} is also double the angle {EAC}.",
              visible: EXT2,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angFEC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angEAC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "EA equals EC, so the angles EAC and ECA are equal", ref: "I.5" },
                  { claim: "FEC is the exterior angle of the triangle EAC",   ref: "I.32" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore the whole angle {BEC} is double the whole angle {BAC}.",
              visible: EXT2,
              highlighted: ["angBEC","angBAC"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the doubles added together", ref: "C.N.2" } ] },
            { text: "Again let another straight line be inflected, and let there be another angle {BDC}.",
              visible: CASE2,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "DB",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "DC",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "angBDC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Join {DE} and produced it to {G}.",
              visible: PROD,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "DG", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "join DE",           ref: "I.Post.1" },
                  { claim: "produce it to G",   ref: "I.Post.2" } ] },
            { text: "Similarly then we can prove that the angle {GEC} is double the angle {EDC},",
              visible: SIM1,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angGEC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angEDC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "ED equals EC",                                  ref: "I.5" },
                  { claim: "GEC is the exterior angle of the triangle EDC", ref: "I.32" } ] },
            { text: "of which the angle {GEB} is double the angle {EDB}.",
              visible: SIM2,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angGEB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angEDB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "ED equals EB",                                  ref: "I.5" },
                  { claim: "GEB is the exterior angle of the triangle EDB", ref: "I.32" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore the remaining angle {BEC} is double the angle {BDC}.",
              visible: SIM2,
              highlighted: ["angBEC","angBDC"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "GEB taken from GEC, EDB from EDC", ref: "C.N.3" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore in a circle the angle at the center is double the angle at the circumference when the angles have the same circumference as base.",
              visible: DONE,
              highlighted: ["angBEC","angBAC","angBDC","arcBC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.20") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Post.2": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post2/",
        "I.Def.15": "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15/",
        "I.5":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI5/",
        "I.32":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI32/",
        "C.N.2":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/",
        "C.N.3":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that the angle {BEC} is double the angle {BAC}.

Join {AE}, and draw it through to {F}.

Then, since {EA} equals {EB}, the angle {EAB} also equals the angle {EBA}. Therefore the sum of the angles the angles {EAB} and {EBA} is double the angle {EAB}. [!just I.5]

But the angle {BEF} equals the sum of the angles {EAB} and {EBA}, therefore the angle {BEF}, is also double the angle {EAB}. [!just I.32]

For the same reason the angle {FEC} is also double the angle {EAC}.

Therefore the whole angle {BEC} is double the whole angle {BAC}.

Again let another straight line be inflected, and let there be another angle {BDC}.  Join {DE} and produced it to {G}.

Similarly then we can prove that the angle {GEC} is double the angle {EDC}, of which the angle {GEB} is double the angle {EDB}. Therefore the remaining angle {BEC} is double the angle {BDC}.

Therefore *in a circle the angle at the center is double the angle at the circumference when the angles have the same circumference as base.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide: This proposition is used in the next one and @III.27.
